hv: map the guest PCIe range as device I/O.

The device-I/O address tier is never set up: res_guest.c doesn't assign the
PCIe range (upstream only does under CONFIG_PCIE), and segments.h's
RANGE_CHECK_IO compiles to a branch-to-fail without it. res_guest.c assigns
the range as IO_SEGMENT in init_guest(); the RANGE_CHECK_IO guard is forced
to #if 1.

Applied through the build's srcfix mechanism, never to the reference tree.

How wide it is, and where it differs from Sun's code:
  * 0xc000000000 .. 0xff00000000 (252 GB) is Sun's own leaf B aperture
    (AID_TO_PCIE_LEAF_B_BASE/LIMIT, the CONFIG_IOBYPASS arm of res_fire_pcie.c),
    not a range chosen here. The guest MD of the pci set declares 256 MB of I/O
    (0xc810000000), about 2 GB of MEM32 (0xca00000000) and about 16 GB of MEM64
    (0xcc00000000): roughly 18 GB, 7% of it. The rest is mapped as I/O too.
  * Sun assigns it from config_a_guest_pcie_bus(), only to a guest that was
    given a PCIe leaf. That code is under CONFIG_PCIE and is not built here, so
    this patch assigns the range to every guest in init_guest(), the legion set
    (no PCIe node in its MD) included.
  * A guest real address inside the range but outside the MD windows is accepted
    by the hypervisor and reaches QEMU, which answers for it; with Sun's own
    gating, or with only the MD windows, the hypervisor would refuse it instead.
    Narrowing needs the windows from the MD at init_guest() time, which this
    build cannot read without the CONFIG_PCIE code; it has not been done.

--- a/common/src/res_guest.c
+++ b/common/src/res_guest.c
@@ -118,6 +118,44 @@
 		init_ra2pa_segment(&(gp->ra2pa_segment[j]));
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * LOCAL DEVIATION (not Sun's source) -- QEMU sun4v PCIe.
+	 *
+	 * A guest real address is only accepted if it falls in a segment of
+	 * this table; anything else makes the TLB miss handler hand the guest
+	 * a data access exception. Memory segments come from the mblocks. The
+	 * PCIe I/O range is assigned in exactly one place upstream --
+	 * greatlakes/ontario/src/res_fire_pcie.c, under CONFIG_PCIE -- so a
+	 * build without it cannot reach device registers at all: Solaris'
+	 * e1000g dies on its first register write, in e1000_reset_hw.
+	 *
+	 * Enabling CONFIG_PCIE is not an option here. It also requires
+	 * CONFIG_FIRE (FIRE_LEAF lives under it, platform/vdev_ops.h:91) and
+	 * so drags in the Fire root complex driver, which programs registers
+	 * QEMU does not model -- and four of the assembly files it needs do
+	 * not build with GNU as at all (cpu_errs.s alone has 159 rejected
+	 * sites).
+	 *
+	 * So assign the range directly, and only the range. The bounds are
+	 * Sun's own: AID_TO_PCIE_LEAF_B_BASE/LIMIT from the CONFIG_IOBYPASS
+	 * arm of res_fire_pcie.c, which is the arm whose addresses match what
+	 * this machine's guest MD describes (0xc810000000 I/O, 0xca00000000
+	 * MEM32, 0xcc00000000 MEM64). The offset is zero: on this machine a
+	 * guest's real addresses equal physical ones.
+	 *
+	 * What this does NOT do is any part of Fire's own setup -- no MSI EQ
+	 * base, no bypass configuration, no error handling. Those live in the
+	 * hypercalls QEMU answers itself.
+	 *
+	 * The flag is IO_SEGMENT, as Sun's own code uses. Setting MEM_SEGMENT
+	 * here instead was tried and stops the hypervisor booting entirely.
+	 * The reason the miss path still refuses such a segment is a test in
+	 * segments.h that looks at MEM_SEGMENT alone; that test is widened in
+	 * override/segments.h, which is where the change belongs.
+	 */
+	assign_ra2pa_segments(gp, 0xc000000000ull,
+	    0xff00000000ull - 0xc000000000ull, 0, IO_SEGMENT);
+
 	asmsg = (hvctl_msg_t *)gp->async_buf;
 	asmsg->hdr.op = HVctl_op_new_res_stat;
 
--- a/common/include/segments.h
+++ b/common/include/segments.h
@@ -268,7 +268,15 @@
 	.poplocals
 /* END CSTYLED */
 
-#ifdef CONFIG_PCIE
+/*
+ * LOCAL OVERRIDE (not Sun's source): was #ifdef CONFIG_PCIE. Without it
+ * RANGE_CHECK_IO compiles to an unconditional branch to the fail label,
+ * which silently deletes the I/O tier of rdmmu_miss (mmu.s:92), dmmu_miss
+ * (:587) and hcall_mmu_map_addr (hcall_mmu.s:513) -- the three places a
+ * guest real address in an IO segment is supposed to be accepted. Those
+ * files compile regardless of CONFIG_PCIE; only this macro vanished.
+ */
+#if 1  /* was: #ifdef CONFIG_PCIE */
 
 /*
  * Note: Could just use RA2PA_RANGE_CONV_UNK_SIZE() macro, but that would
